    "responsePreview": "Assuming an `orders` table with a `customer_id` and an order timestamp, here's the query:\n\n```sql\nSELECT *\nFROM orders\nWHERE customer_id = :customer_id\nORDER BY created_at DESC\nLIMIT 10;\n```\n\nNotes:\n- `:customer_id` is a placeholder — bind your actual customer ID (or `?` / `$1` depending on your driver).\n- `created_at` should be the column that represents when the order was placed. If your table uses a different name (e.g. `order_date`, `placed_at`), swap it in.\n- Add a tie-breaker such as `, id DESC` to `ORDER BY` if multiple orders can share the same timestamp and you need a stable order.\n- On SQL Server / older engines that lack `LIMIT`:\n\n```sql\n-- SQL Server\nSELECT TOP 10 *\nFROM orders\nWHERE customer_id = :customer_id\nORDER BY created_at DESC;\n\n-- Oracle (12c+)\nSELECT *\nFROM orders\nWHERE customer_id = :customer_id\nORDER BY created_at DESC\nFETCH FIRST 10 ROWS ONLY;\n```\n\nIf you share your actual table/column names, I can tailor it exactly.",
